e continued personal attacks are especially disappointing.

Andrew

________________________________
From: Ken Kubota <ietf@kenkubota.de>
Sent: Thursday, October 15, 2026 8:22 am
To: Jan Schaumann <jschauma@netmeister.org>
Cc: ietf@ietf.org <ietf@ietf.org>; tls@ietf.org <tls@ietf.org>; ietf-discus=
sion@listi.jpberlin.de <ietf-discussion@listi.jpberlin.de>
Subject: Re: [TLS] NSA / IETF Discussion List

My responses are provided in line with the quoted text below.


Am 14.10.2026 um 16:02 schrieb Jan Schaumann <jschauma@netmeister.org>:

Ken Kubota <ietf@kenkubota.de> wrote:
For those who disagree with NSA activities at the IETF or wish to find solu=
tions for the issues concerning the current IETF situation, you can join th=
is mailing list:

https://listi.jpberlin.de/mailman/listinfo/ietf-discussion

This list is not hosted by the IETF, so could you
clarify what the scope and affiliation is?

The scope was intentionally kept open to encourage people to join and contr=
ibute their ideas, wishes, and visions for the future.
Although the NSA's current activities at the IETF (NSA's SIGINT Enabling Pr=
oject [1]) were the catalyst for creating this mailing list, numerous other=
 issues within the IETF remain unaddressed.
A significant discrepancy exists between the IETF's stated principles and i=
ts actual practices.
The internet, and hence the IETF, should serve the end user, as outlined in=
 an RFC [2].
In reality, however, the IETF appears to serve large U.S. companies whose i=
nterests mirror those of the NSA, such as by erroneously asserting a "rough=
 consensus" [3] at the TLS Working Group in a decision which effectively we=
akens internet encryption (Professor Bernstein: "But there wasn't consensus=
; not even close. There also wasn't "rough consensus"." [4]).
The leading German computer magazine already published an article concernin=
g the issue of whether the "IETF standardization [is] easy to hijack". [5]
In my opinion, the answer is clearly yes, and moreover, it is evident to me=
 that the IETF (or the IETF decision-making process) as a whole has been co=
mpromised/hijacked. [6]
Please also read the "Background" links at the mailing list website for mor=
e information.
The internet, and consequently internet standardization organisations, shou=
ld serve rather the end user than large companies, and in a multipolar worl=
d, the equality of peoples, and the equality of languages, should be envisi=
oned instead of U.S. primacy / hegemony. China, Russia, and India, and thei=
r languages, should play a larger role.
Bitcoin (or other means of decentralized payment methods) bypassing central=
 banking is important, but seems to have no place in the IETF.
Although the IETF once undoubtedly delivered valuable outcomes, it has unfo=
rtunately become a liability.
Overall, the prevailing sentiment is that certain organizations operate mor=
e as gatekeepers than enablers.

Another purpose of the new mailing list is to bypass IETF censorship, as di=
scussed further below.

The new mailing list is a private initiative, which means that there is no =
affiliation.
However, this doesn't exclude the possibility that from the new mailing lis=
t's activity something new will arise, might it be an informal structure or=
 an official organization.
Of course, compliance with privacy laws will be ensured; no data will be pa=
ssed on to third parties (including a potential future legal entity) withou=
t explicit permission or legal requirement.
On the mailing list website it is clearly announced that mails are availabl=
e publicly at the mailing list archive (similar to IETF standards).


I.e., shouldn't discussions about the IETF happen on
IETF lists?

Ideally, this would be the case.
However, there is a significant discrepancy between what the IETF promises =
and what it actually does.
Professor Bernstein has made this explicit in the case of the SSH Working G=
roup chaired by Stephen Farrell and another person: "1. What IETF promises"=
 vs. "2. Chairs sabotaging participation" or "3. Chairs sabotaging resoluti=
on of technical disputes" [7]: "issuing continual threats of censorship, pr=
esumably scaring many WG participants into silence" [7].
In my opinion, the same holds for the TLS Working Group ("The claim of a "r=
ough consensus" was made by a WG Chair composed of three members. One of th=
e TLS WG chairs works for an NSA-related company, while another one works f=
or a CIA-funded company.") [3].
Nobel laureate Paul Romer already mentioned that the TLS group discussion i=
s dysfunctional, and people "seem to be afraid to speak up about the mistak=
es": "I share the concern that many members voice: the group discussion is =
dysfunctional." [8] "The deep problem is the missing messages. People seem =
to be afraid to speak up about the mistakes." [9]
Moreover, there is a tendency at the IETF towards total censorship, as Prof=
essor Bernstein pointed out: "2025.10.05: MODPOD: The collapse of IETF's pr=
otections for dissent. #ietf #objections #censorship #hybrids" [10], see al=
so [11].
In an absurd decision, the TLS WG has "decided to moderate the postings of =
D. J. Bernstein" [12], whose Curve25519 (X25519 [13]) probably runs 90% of =
today's internet worldwide [14].
In my case, former NSA lifetime employee Deb Cooley has pleaded for removin=
g my publishing rights [15], based on a request by John Mattsson from Erics=
son (Swedish Wallenberg family). "They have to firmly align themselves with=
 the Washington agenda in order to keep Ericsson and Saab profitable." [16]
The Orwellian state of affairs couldn't be more obvious.
Any discussion of the conflict of interest is framed as and blocked as "per=
sonal attack."

Please direct further questions to the new mailing list, as it is an evolvi=
ng process where everybody may participate freely.
I also would like to note upfront that answers from my side may take a whil=
e.


Kind regards,

Ken Kubota

____________________________________________________

Ken Kubota
