<div dir=3D"auto">Am 14.10.2026 um 16:02 schrieb Jan Schaumann &lt;jschauma=
@netmeister.org&gt;:</div>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Ken Kubota &lt;ietf@kenkubota.de&gt; wrote:</div>
<blockquote>
<div dir=3D"auto">For those who disagree with NSA activities at the IETF or=
 wish to find solutions for the issues concerning the current IETF situatio=
n, you can join this mailing list:<br>
<br>
https://listi.jpberlin.de/mailman/listinfo/ietf-discussion</div>
</blockquote>
<div dir=3D"auto"><br>
This list is not hosted by the IETF, so could you<br>
clarify what the scope and affiliation is?</div>
</blockquote>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">The scope was intentionally kept open to encourage people=
 to join and contribute their ideas, wishes, and visions for the future.</d=
iv>
<div dir=3D"auto">Although the NSA's current activities at the IETF (NSA's =
SIGINT Enabling Project [1]) were the catalyst for creating this mailing li=
st, numerous other issues within the IETF remain unaddressed.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">A significant discrepancy exists between the IETF's state=
d principles and its actual practices.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">The internet, and hence the IETF, should serve the end us=
er, as outlined in an RFC [2].</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">In reality, however, the IETF appears to serve large U.S.=
 companies whose interests mirror those of the NSA, such as by erroneously =
asserting a &quot;rough consensus&quot; [3] at the TLS Working Group in a d=
ecision which effectively weakens internet encryption
 (Professor Bernstein: &quot;But there wasn't consensus; not even close. Th=
ere also wasn't &quot;rough consensus&quot;.&quot; [4]).</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">The leading German computer magazine already published an=
 article concerning the issue of whether the &quot;IETF standardization [is=
] easy to hijack&quot;. [5]</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">In my opinion, the answer is clearly yes, and moreover, i=
t is evident to me that the IETF (or the IETF decision-making process) as a=
 whole has&nbsp;been compromised/hijacked. [6]</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Please also read the &quot;Background&quot; links at the =
mailing list website for more information.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">The internet, and consequently internet standardization o=
rganisations, should serve rather the end user than large companies, and in=
 a multipolar world, the equality of peoples, and the equality of languages=
, should be envisioned instead of
 U.S. primacy / hegemony. China, Russia, and India, and their languages, sh=
ould play a larger role.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Bitcoin (or other means of decentralized payment methods)=
 bypassing central banking is important, but seems to have no place in the =
IETF.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Although the IETF once undoubtedly delivered valuable out=
comes, it has unfortunately become a liability.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Overall, the prevailing sentiment is that certain organiz=
ations operate more as gatekeepers than enablers.</div>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Another purpose of the new mailing list is to bypass IETF=
 censorship, as discussed further below.</div>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">The new mailing list is a private initiative, which means=
 that there is no affiliation.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">However, this doesn't exclude the possibility that from t=
he new mailing list's activity something new will arise, might it be an inf=
ormal structure or an official organization.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Of course, compliance with privacy laws will be ensured; =
no data will be passed on to third parties (including a potential future le=
gal entity) without explicit permission or legal requirement.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">On the mailing list website it is clearly announced that =
mails are available publicly at the mailing list archive (similar to IETF s=
tandards).</div>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<blockquote>
<div dir=3D"auto">I.e., shouldn't discussions about the IETF happen on<br>
IETF lists?</div>
</blockquote>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Ideally, this would be the case.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">However, there is a significant discrepancy between what =
the IETF promises and what it actually does.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Professor Bernstein has made this explicit in the case of=
 the SSH Working Group chaired by Stephen Farrell and another person: &quot=
;1. What IETF promises&quot; vs. &quot;2. Chairs sabotaging participation&q=
uot; or &quot;3. Chairs sabotaging resolution of technical
 disputes&quot; [7]: &quot;issuing continual threats of censorship, presuma=
bly scaring many WG participants into silence&quot; [7].</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">In my opinion, the same holds for the TLS Working Group (=
&quot;The claim of a &quot;rough consensus&quot; was made by a WG Chair com=
posed of three members. One of the TLS WG chairs works for an NSA-related c=
ompany, while another one works for a CIA-funded
 company.&quot;) [3].</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Nobel laureate Paul Romer already mentioned that the TLS =
group discussion is dysfunctional, and people &quot;seem to be afraid to sp=
eak up about the mistakes&quot;: &quot;I share the concern that many member=
s voice: the group discussion is dysfunctional.&quot;
 [8] &quot;The deep problem is the missing messages. People seem to be afra=
id to speak up about the mistakes.&quot; [9]</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Moreover, there is a tendency at the IETF towards total c=
ensorship, as Professor Bernstein pointed out: &quot;2025.10.05: MODPOD: Th=
e collapse of IETF's protections for dissent. #ietf #objections #censorship=
 #hybrids&quot; [10], see also [11].</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">In an absurd decision, the TLS WG has &quot;decided to mo=
derate the postings of D. J. Bernstein&quot; [12], whose Curve25519 (X25519=
 [13]) probably runs 90% of today's internet worldwide [14].</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">In my case, former NSA lifetime employee Deb Cooley has p=
leaded for removing my publishing rights [15], based on a request by John M=
attsson from Ericsson (Swedish Wallenberg family). &quot;They have to firml=
y align themselves with the Washington
 agenda in order to keep Ericsson and Saab profitable.&quot; [16]</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">The Orwellian state of affairs couldn't be more obvious.<=
/div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Any discussion of the conflict of interest is framed as a=
nd blocked as &quot;personal attack.&quot;</div>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Please direct further questions to the new mailing list, =
as it is an evolving process where everybody may participate freely.</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">I also would like to note upfront that answers from my si=
de may take a while.</div>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<div dir=3D"ltr"><br>
</div>
<div dir=3D"auto">Kind regards,<br>
<br>
Ken Kubota<br>
<br>
